// (3.7.2) Each catchable animal in a few sentences, as it is in the real world (the legendary giants and the
// undocumented species as they are in this sea): shown under the specimen station's aquarium. Keyed by species id;
// anything missing falls back to its one-line description.
export const ABOUT = {
  1: "Lives inside a host anemone and almost never strays more than a metre from it. A layer of mucus keeps the anemone from stinging it. Every clownfish starts life male; when the female of a pair dies, the largest male changes sex and takes her place.",
  2: "A surgeonfish, named for the sharp spine on each side of the tail, which it can flick out like a scalpel. Juveniles hide in branching coral and wedge themselves in when threatened. Adults roam the reef face in loose groups, grazing on plankton and algae.",
  3: "Almost all of the yellow tangs in the aquarium trade come from the reefs off Hawaii. They graze algae through the day in small groups and turn duller, with a white stripe, at night. Their spines near the tail are sharp enough to cut a careless hand.",
  4: "Looks like a butterflyfish but has a family to itself. It eats mostly sponges, which makes it one of the hardest reef fish to keep alive in captivity. The long white streamer off its dorsal fin can be longer than the fish.",
  5: "Lives on Caribbean and western Atlantic reefs, where sponges make up most of its diet. The blue-ringed spot on its forehead is the crown it is named for. Pairs stay together and often patrol the same stretch of reef for years.",
  6: "The juveniles are so different from the adults, navy blue with white rings, that they were once thought to be a separate species. The change to yellow and blue stripes takes about two years. Adults make a low knocking sound when disturbed.",
  7: "Uses its long beak to pick small worms and shrimp out of holes in the coral. The black spot near its tail looks like an eye, and a predator lunging at the wrong end gives it a chance to escape. Pairs usually forage together.",
  8: "One of the most widespread butterflyfish, found from the Red Sea to Hawaii. The thin thread off the back of the dorsal fin is where it gets its name. It feeds on coral polyps, small worms and algae, usually in pairs.",
  9: "Rarely bigger than a thumb. It spends the day hidden in rubble and comes out at dusk, when pairs rise off the bottom together to spawn. It has no scales; instead its skin is covered in a foul-tasting mucus that keeps most predators away.",
  10: "Native to the Indo-Pacific, it was released into the Atlantic in the 1980s and has since spread across the Caribbean, where nothing hunts it. Its fin spines carry a venom that is painful but rarely dangerous to people. It corners small fish with its fan-like fins and swallows them whole.",
  11: "Sits motionless on the bottom looking exactly like a lump of rock or coral. The spines along its back carry the strongest venom of any known fish, and stepping on one is excruciating. It can survive out of water for hours on a falling tide.",
  12: "An ambush hunter with a fishing lure on its head, a modified fin ray it wiggles over its mouth. When something comes close it opens its mouth so fast the prey is sucked in before it can react. It can swallow fish nearly as large as itself.",
  13: "A Caribbean grazer that scrapes algae off dead coral with fused, beak-like teeth. In doing so it grinds up so much rock that it passes it out as fine white sand. Like many parrotfish, it starts life female and the largest females later turn into brightly coloured males.",
  14: "The largest parrotfish in the world. Herds of them graze reef flats at dawn, biting off chunks of living coral with their beaks, and use the bump on their heads to break coral apart. It is heavily fished and now rare in much of its range.",
  15: "Can grow as large as a person and live for thirty years. Its thick lips are thought to protect it from the spines and venom of its prey, which include crown-of-thorns starfish, boxfish and sea urchins. It is curious around divers and often follows them across the reef.",
  16: "Sets up a cleaning station on the reef, where larger fish line up and hold still to have parasites and dead skin picked off them. Even groupers and moray eels let it swim inside their mouths. Another fish, the false cleanerfish, mimics its colours to get close enough to bite.",
  17: "A small Caribbean fish that hovers under ledges and in caves, often swimming upside down along the roof. The male builds a nest from bits of algae and guards the eggs. Despite its size, it will defend its patch of reef against much larger fish.",
  18: "Encased in a rigid box of fused bony plates, with holes for its eyes, mouth and fins. When stressed it releases a toxin from its skin that can kill other fish in a small space, including itself. It swims slowly by sculling its fins.",
  19: "When threatened it gulps water until it swells into a ball two or three times its size, and its long spines stand straight out. It feeds at night on snails, crabs and sea urchins, crushing them with its beak. Its large eyes are unusually good for a reef fish.",
  20: "Lives in very shallow water over reef flats and lagoons, and guards its territory fiercely. Its Hawaiian name, humuhumunukunukuapua'a, is one of the longest fish names in use. Like all triggerfish it can lock its first dorsal spine upright to wedge itself into a crack.",
  21: "The largest triggerfish on the reef, and the one divers fear most. During nesting it guards a cone-shaped area rising from the nest to the surface, and will charge and bite anyone who enters it. It flips over rocks to get at urchins and crabs underneath.",
  22: "Its bold white spots on a dark belly are thought to break up its outline when seen from below. It is shy and aggressive at once, avoiding divers but attacking other fish that come near its hole. It eats crabs, snails and urchins, and can crack hard shells with its teeth.",
  23: "One of the largest morays in the Atlantic. Its skin is actually brown; the green colour comes from a layer of yellowish mucus over it. It breathes by opening and closing its mouth, which makes it look more threatening than it usually is.",
  24: "The bulkiest moray eel, sometimes over 30 kg. In the Red Sea it has been seen hunting with roving coral groupers, the grouper flushing fish out of the open and the eel chasing them into crevices. It has a second set of jaws in its throat that pulls prey down.",
  25: "Begins life as a black male with a yellow fin, turns blue and yellow as it matures, and finally becomes an all-yellow female. It spends most of its life with its body in a sand burrow, only the head sticking out. The flared nostrils are sensitive organs for smell.",
  26: "Lives in colonies of hundreds on sandy slopes, each eel in its own burrow with just the front of its body swaying in the current, catching plankton. If a diver gets close, the whole field sinks back into the sand. They hardly ever leave the burrow at all.",
  27: "A bold Caribbean damselfish named for the stripes on its sides, which resemble an army sergeant's insignia. Males turn bluish while guarding patches of purple eggs on rocks and chase off much larger fish. They are common around docks and pilings.",
  28: "Gathers in loose clouds above the reef during the day, picking plankton out of the water. At the first sign of danger the whole group drops back into the coral. It is one of the most common fish over Caribbean reefs.",
  29: "Named for the grunting sound it makes by rubbing its throat teeth together. It schools by day in the shade of coral heads and spreads out over sand and seagrass at night to feed. Two grunts facing off often press their open mouths together, a display that looks like kissing.",
  30: "Lives around reefs, wrecks and the legs of oil platforms in the Gulf of Mexico. Big females can be over fifty years old and lay millions of eggs a season. It was so heavily fished in the twentieth century that catches are now tightly controlled.",
  31: "Gathers in thousands at a handful of spawning sites around the full moons of winter. Fishermen learned where and when, and many of those gatherings have disappeared. It can change from barred to pale to almost black in seconds.",
  32: "The largest bony fish found on coral reefs, reaching close to 400 kg. It hunts spiny lobsters, rays, turtles and even small sharks, swallowing them whole. Very old individuals often stay in one cave or wreck for years.",
  33: "A huge grouper of the western Atlantic that can weigh as much as a small car. It makes a deep booming sound with its swim bladder when disturbed, which divers can feel as much as hear. After years of protection, numbers are slowly recovering.",
  34: "An ambush hunter that waits beside coral heads for small fish to come within striking distance. It sometimes hunts with moray eels or octopuses, using them to flush prey out. Like many groupers, it begins life female and some later turn male.",
  35: "Hangs nearly motionless in the water and then strikes with a burst of speed. It is attracted to anything shiny and has bitten swimmers wearing jewellery by mistake. Large barracuda from some reefs carry ciguatera toxin and are unsafe to eat.",
  36: "A thick, powerful jack that hunts in packs along reef edges. In the Seychelles, giant trevally have been filmed leaping out of the water to catch young seabirds in flight. Fishermen prize it for the strength of its runs.",
  37: "The blue fins and dark speckles make it easy to recognise. It often follows octopuses, eels and goatfish as they forage, snatching anything that bolts. It is one of the most common predators on Pacific reefs.",
  38: "Has a swim bladder that works partly like a lung, so it can gulp air at the surface and survive in warm, low-oxygen water. Its big silver scales were once sold as souvenirs. Hooked tarpon leap clear of the water, which makes them famous with anglers.",
  39: "Keeps its muscles warmer than the water around it, which lets it hunt in cold seas and cross whole oceans. The largest on record weighed close to 700 kg. A single fish has sold for over three million dollars at auction in Tokyo.",
  40: "Fast, schooling tuna of warm seas, named for its long yellow fins. In the eastern Pacific, schools often travel with dolphins, which led to large numbers of dolphins drowning in tuna nets before the practice was changed. It can reach 200 kg.",
  41: "The smallest of the commercial tunas and the one most of the world's canned tuna comes from. It grows quickly and breeds year-round, which lets it stand up to heavy fishing. Schools gather under drifting logs and debris.",
  42: "Its long, flat bill is used to slash through schools of fish rather than to spear them. A special heating organ near its eyes keeps its brain and eyes warm while it hunts deep, cold water at night. Adults have no scales and no teeth.",
  43: "Can raise its huge dorsal fin like a sail to look bigger while herding sardines into tight balls. Groups of them take turns slashing through the school with their bills. It changes colour rapidly while hunting, flashing stripes along its side.",
  44: "One of the largest bony fish, with females much bigger than males. It hunts tuna and squid, stunning them with blows from its bill. Its sides light up with blue bars when it is excited.",
  45: "Lives near the surface in warm seas and gathers under anything floating, from weed lines to logs. It grows very fast and rarely lives more than five years. Its colours fade from gold and green to grey within minutes of death.",
  46: "The heaviest bony fish, sometimes over 2,000 kg, mostly made of a huge head and two tall fins. It feeds largely on jellyfish and spends time lying flat at the surface, possibly to warm up and to let seabirds pick parasites off it. A single female can carry hundreds of millions of eggs.",
  47: "Builds up speed under the surface, then launches into the air and glides on its wide pectoral fins. Each glide can carry it fifty metres or more, and it can extend the flight by beating its tail against the water. It is a common sight from ships in warm seas.",
  48: "Forms some of the largest schools of any animal, sometimes billions strong. Almost everything in the North Atlantic eats herring, from cod and puffins to humpback whales. Herring communicate in part by releasing bubbles from their swim bladders.",
  49: "Feeds on plankton near the surface along the coasts of the Pacific and southern Africa. In South Africa its winter migration, the sardine run, draws in dolphins, sharks, gannets and whales. Sardine populations rise and crash with changes in ocean temperature.",
  50: "A small, short-lived fish of the California Current that filters plankton through its gill rakers. It is a key food for sea lions, pelicans and larger fish. Numbers swing wildly from year to year with ocean conditions.",
  51: "Has no swim bladder, so it must keep swimming to avoid sinking. It migrates in large schools between feeding and spawning grounds around the North Atlantic. Its fatty flesh spoils quickly, which is why it is often smoked or salted.",
  52: "One of the fastest fish in the sea, with a long body, a pointed beak of a jaw and razor-edged teeth. It hunts alone or in small groups in open tropical water. It is prized by sport fishermen for its first run, which can strip a reel in seconds.",
  53: "A round, flat fish the size of a car tyre, red with white spots. In 2015 it was found to warm its whole body by flapping its fins, which makes it the first fish known to be fully warm-blooded. That warmth lets it stay fast and alert while hunting squid in cold water.",
  54: "The longest bony fish known, reaching more than eight metres, though it is rarely seen alive. It swims by rippling the long red fin down its back while holding its body vertical. Most of what is known about oarfish comes from dying ones washed ashore, which are probably behind many sea-serpent stories. This one is about as long as they get.",
  55: "Hatches in rivers, spends one to four years feeding at sea, then finds its way back to the stream where it was born, probably by smell. Unlike Pacific salmon, it does not always die after spawning and some return to the sea to spawn again. Wild populations have fallen sharply over the past century.",
  56: "The largest Pacific salmon, sometimes over 50 kg, called king salmon in North America. Like other Pacific salmon it spawns once and then dies, and its body feeds the stream, the bears and the forest around it. Southern Resident orcas depend on it as their main food.",
  57: "Feeds at sea mostly on plankton, which is why its flesh is such a deep red. When it returns to its home lake system to spawn, its body turns red and its head green, and males grow a hooked jaw and a hump. Bristol Bay in Alaska has the largest sockeye runs in the world.",
  58: "Lives along the Atlantic coast and moves into rivers to spawn. It was introduced to the Pacific coast in the 1870s by train, carried across the country in milk cans. Large females can live for thirty years.",
  59: "Stalks its prey from straight ahead, where its very thin body is nearly invisible, then shoots out its jaw to snatch it. The dark spot on each side is said to be St Peter's thumbprint. It is a fine eating fish in Europe despite its looks.",
  60: "Fed much of Europe for centuries, and its fishery helped draw Europeans to North America. The Grand Banks stocks off Newfoundland collapsed in the early 1990s and have still not fully recovered. It can live more than twenty years and grow to 90 kg.",
  61: "A cod relative of the North Atlantic, told apart by the black thumbprint mark above its pectoral fin. It feeds on worms, brittle stars and shellfish on the seabed. In Britain it is the classic fish for fish and chips in the north.",
  62: "Forms vast schools in the Bering Sea and the North Pacific, and is the largest single-species fishery in the world. Most fish sticks, imitation crab and fast-food fish sandwiches are made from it. Young pollock are a main food for Steller sea lions and fur seals.",
  63: "The largest flatfish in the world. It starts life swimming upright like any fish, then one eye migrates over the top of its head and it settles on its side. It is a strong swimmer for a flatfish and hunts fish well off the bottom.",
  64: "Lies flat on the bottom of the North Pacific, its dark upper side matching the seabed, and ambushes fish and crabs that come near. Females grow much larger than males and can weigh over 200 kg. Some live for more than fifty years.",
  65: "A common flatfish of European coasts, brown with bright orange spots on its eyed side. It feeds mostly at night on worms and small clams and spends the day buried in sand. Its orange spots stay even as it changes its shading to match the bottom.",
  66: "A large, almost round flatfish with both eyes on its left side. Instead of scales it has scattered bony knobs on its upper surface. It is farmed in Europe and fetches high prices in restaurants.",
  67: "Lies almost buried on the seabed with a fleshy lure on a long spine waving above its huge mouth. It will swallow almost anything that comes close, including diving seabirds. The tail is the only part usually eaten, and is sometimes called poor man's lobster.",
  68: "Not a cod at all but a greenling, with a large mouth full of sharp teeth. About one in five has blue-green flesh, coloured by a bile pigment, which turns white when cooked. Males guard the egg masses for weeks and will bite divers who come too close.",
  69: "One of the longest-lived fish known, with some individuals over a century old. It grows slowly and bears live young after the eggs hatch inside the mother. Because it matures so late, overfishing hit its populations hard and they recover slowly.",
  70: "Lives in rocky crevices in the cold North Atlantic and crushes crabs, urchins and whelks with its heavy canine teeth and molars. Its blood carries antifreeze proteins that stop it from freezing in near-zero water. It replaces its teeth every year.",
  71: "A wrasse of the California kelp forests. Every sheephead is born female, and the largest ones later turn into males with black heads and tails and a red belt. It crushes sea urchins, which helps keep the kelp from being grazed away.",
  72: "The state marine fish of California and protected there. Males clear a patch of red algae on a rock and guard it fiercely, inviting females in to lay eggs. It will make an audible thump to warn divers away from its nest.",
  73: "Lives on the muddy slopes of the North Pacific down to almost 3,000 metres. Its oily, rich flesh is sold as black cod. Young fish live near the surface and only move into deep water as they grow.",
  74: "Gathers in dense schools over seamounts and deep ridges, where it was fished heavily from the 1980s. It grows very slowly and can live for well over a hundred years, so populations take decades to recover. Its bright orange colour looks dull grey at the depth where it lives.",
  75: "A large predator of the cold seas around Antarctica, sold in restaurants as Chilean sea bass. It can live for fifty years and grow over 2 metres. Illegal fishing for it was rampant in the 1990s before stronger controls came in.",
  76: "The largest predatory fish, reaching 6 metres. It ambushes seals from below at speed, sometimes launching its whole body out of the water. It can sense tiny electric fields from muscles through pores on its snout.",
  77: "The largest fish in the world, sometimes over 18 metres. It feeds by filtering plankton, fish eggs and small fish through its gills, and is harmless to people. Each one has a unique pattern of spots, which researchers use to identify individuals.",
  78: "Eats nearly anything it comes across, from sea turtles and seabirds to rubbish dropped from boats. Its broad, serrated teeth can cut through turtle shell. Young sharks have dark stripes that fade as they grow.",
  79: "The wide head spreads out its sense organs, helping it find stingrays buried in sand. By day, hundreds gather in schools around seamounts and islands, then scatter to hunt alone at night. It is now endangered after heavy fishing for its fins.",
  80: "A small shark of shallow reefs and lagoons, often seen with its black-tipped dorsal fin above the water. It feeds on reef fish, squid and crabs. It is timid with people but sometimes nips at feet in very shallow water.",
  81: "Spends the day resting on the bottom under ledges, often piled on top of other nurse sharks. At night it hunts by sucking prey out of holes with a force strong enough to pull a conch from its shell. Unlike most sharks it can pump water over its gills while lying still.",
  82: "The fastest shark, capable of short bursts over 70 km/h, and one of the few that leaps clear of the water. It hunts tuna and swordfish and keeps its body warmer than the sea. Its long, curved teeth are visible even with its mouth shut.",
  83: "One of the most widely travelled sharks, crossing whole oceans on regular migrations. It has long, wing-like pectoral fins and a deep indigo back. Millions are caught every year, mostly as bycatch on longlines.",
  84: "The second-largest fish, cruising slowly near the surface with its mouth open wide enough to swallow a small car. It filters plankton through gill rakers and is harmless to people. It disappears into deep water in winter, and where it goes was a mystery until satellite tags followed it.",
  85: "Probably the longest-lived vertebrate known: radiocarbon dating of their eye lenses suggests some are over 400 years old. It moves extremely slowly in near-freezing water and still manages to catch seals, perhaps while they sleep. A parasitic copepod often hangs from its eyes.",
  86: "A pink, flabby shark from the deep with a long, flat snout full of sensory pores. When it bites, its whole jaw shoots forward from the head on elastic ligaments. Few have ever been seen alive, and this one is larger than any on record.",
  87: "An eel-like shark with six frilled gill slits and rows of tiny, three-pointed teeth, built to snag squid. Its body plan has barely changed in eighty million years. It may carry its young for more than three years, the longest pregnancy of any animal.",
  88: "First discovered in 1976, when one tangled in a US Navy anchor off Hawaii. It filter-feeds on krill with a huge, rubbery mouth and follows the plankton up toward the surface at night. Fewer than three hundred have ever been recorded.",
  89: "One of the oldest shark lineages, with six gill slits where most sharks have five. It spends the day in deep water and rises at night to hunt fish, seals and other sharks. Its large eyes glow green in a diver's light.",
  90: "A small shark with a glowing belly and a strange way of feeding: it latches onto much larger animals with its lips and spins, cutting out a round plug of flesh. Whales, tuna and dolphins carry its crater-shaped scars. It has even bitten the rubber sonar domes of submarines.",
  91: "The largest ray, with a wingspan of up to seven metres. It filters plankton with its mouth wide open and visits cleaning stations on reefs where small fish pick it clean. It has the biggest brain relative to size of any fish.",
  92: "A ray with a duck-like snout and a back covered in white spots. It flies through the water in groups and often leaps clear of the surface. Its jaws are plates that crush clams and snails dug out of the sand.",
  93: "Lies buried in sand with only its eyes showing, and digs for buried clams and worms by blowing water at the bottom. The venomous barb on its tail is used only in defence. At Stingray City in the Cayman Islands they gather in hundreds to be fed by tourists.",
  94: "A small ray of Indo-Pacific reefs, often seen half hidden under coral ledges. Its bright blue spots are a warning: the tail carries one or two venomous spines. It moves into shallow sand flats with the rising tide to hunt crabs and worms.",
  95: "Hunts by wrapping its wings around a fish and delivering a shock of up to 45 volts. The electric organs in its round body make up a large share of its weight. Divers who touch one get a jolt strong enough to knock them back.",
  96: "Has a long, flat snout edged with teeth on both sides, used to slash through schools of fish and to dig in the mud. The young are born with their saws covered in a soft sheath so they do not injure the mother. Once common along the coast of Florida, it is now critically endangered.",
  97: "A chimaera, a cousin of sharks that split from them about 400 million years ago. It glides slowly over the bottom using its large pectoral fins and has a venomous spine in front of its dorsal fin. Its big green eyes reflect light like a cat's.",
  98: "One of the most numerous fish in the world, though few people have heard of it. Lanternfish spend the day in deep water and rise to the surface at night to feed, a daily migration that moves billions of tonnes of life. The pattern of lights along its belly is different for each species.",
  99: "Its body is thin as a coin and its sides are mirrors, so it is very hard to see in open water. Lights along its belly match the faint glow from above, which hides its silhouette from predators looking up. Its eyes point upward to spot the outline of food against the light.",
  100: "The humpback anglerfish is normally no bigger than a fist, black and round with a glowing lure on a rod above its mouth. The light comes from bacteria living in the lure. Males are tiny and short-lived; the females do the hunting. This one is nearly two metres long, which no anglerfish has ever been known to reach.",
  101: "A small, heavy-headed fish whose fangs are so long that it has pockets in the roof of its mouth to hold them when it closes. It lives deeper than almost any other fish of its size, down to 5,000 metres. Young fangtooths look so different from adults they were once described as a separate species. This one is the size of a man.",
  102: "Its fangs are too long to fit inside its mouth and curve up in front of its eyes. It lures prey with a glowing tip on the first ray of its dorsal fin, then strikes with its head thrown back. Like many deep fish, it migrates toward the surface at night.",
  103: "Females are long, black and toothy with a glowing barbel on the chin, while males are small, brown and have no teeth or working gut at all. The larvae have their eyes on long stalks. It hunts small fish and crustaceans in the dark middle layers of the ocean.",
  104: "Its head is a clear, fluid-filled dome, and the green tubes inside are its eyes, which can rotate to look up through the dome or forward toward its mouth. It probably steals food from the tentacles of siphonophores. The first good look at a live one came only in 2009, and this one is far larger than any yet seen.",
  105: "Almost all mouth: its loose jaws open into a pouch bigger than the rest of its body, used to engulf small shrimp and fish. Its tail ends in a glowing organ that may serve as a lure. The usual adult is under a metre long. This one is more than double that.",
  106: "Has an elastic stomach that lets it swallow fish more than twice its length and ten times its weight. Sometimes the meal is too big and starts to rot before it can be digested, and the gas floats the swallower to the surface. That is how most specimens have been found.",
  107: "Looks like an ordinary fish at depth, where its gelatinous body is held in shape by the pressure. Brought up quickly, it sags into the droopy blob that made it famous as the world's ugliest animal. It hovers over the seabed and eats whatever drifts into its mouth. This one is several times the usual size.",
  108: "Known only from fossils and thought to have died out with the dinosaurs, until a fisherman caught one off South Africa in 1938. It has fleshy, limb-like fins that move in the same pattern as a walking animal's legs. It can live for a century and gives birth to live young.",
  109: "One of the largest grenadiers, or rattails, with a big head and a body that tapers to a thin point. It cruises above the bottom on the continental slope of the North Pacific. Its flesh is so watery that it has never been much good for food.",
  110: "Stands on the bottom on three long, stiff fin rays, two from the pelvic fins and one from the tail, and faces into the current. It holds its pectoral fins out in front like antennae to feel for small animals drifting past. Each one is both male and female.",
  111: "Has no jaws and no bones and is nearly blind. When grabbed, it releases slime that swells into litres of goo in seconds, clogging the gills of anything that bit it. It can tie its body in a knot and slide the knot along itself to scrape off slime or tear food.",
  112: "Grips seagrass and sponges with its tail and sucks up small shrimp through its snout. As in all seahorses, the male carries the eggs in a pouch until they hatch. Pairs greet each other with a little dance each morning.",
  113: "One of the largest seahorses, found around southern Australia and New Zealand. Males have a large, pale belly pouch where females lay their eggs. It is one of the few seahorses that does well in captivity.",
  114: "Smaller than a fingernail and covered in knobs that match the colour and texture of the gorgonian sea fan it lives on. It was only discovered when a scientist found one on a sea fan collected for a museum. Each pair stays on a single fan for life.",
  115: "Covered in leaf-shaped flaps of skin that make it look like a drifting piece of seaweed. It moves very slowly, using tiny clear fins on its neck and back. It lives only off southern Australia and is fully protected there.",
  116: "A seadragon of southern Australian reefs, red with yellow spots and blue bars, with smaller weed-like flaps than its leafy cousin. Males carry the eggs on the underside of the tail. It feeds on tiny shrimp called mysids.",
  117: "A relative of seahorses with a straight body ringed in red and white. It often acts as a cleaner, picking parasites off larger fish. Pairs stay close to caves and ledges on Indo-Pacific reefs.",
  134: "The largest clam, sometimes over a metre across and 200 kg. Its colourful mantle is full of single-celled algae that make food from sunlight, which feeds the clam. The stories of divers trapped by one are almost certainly myths, since it closes too slowly.",
  135: "Anchors itself to rocks with strong threads called byssus, and forms dense beds on rocky shores. A single mussel filters several litres of water an hour. It is farmed widely in Europe and North America.",
  136: "Builds reefs as new oysters settle on old shells, and those reefs shelter fish and crabs. An adult can filter over 100 litres of water a day. Chesapeake Bay once had so many that they were said to filter the whole bay in a week.",
  137: "Has a row of bright blue eyes along the edge of its mantle, each with a tiny mirror inside. It swims by clapping its two shells together, jetting water out in a zigzag escape from starfish. The part people eat is the big muscle that closes the shell.",
  138: "A large sea snail of Caribbean seagrass beds, famous for the flared pink lip of its shell. It grazes on algae and moves in short hops using a claw-like foot. It is heavily harvested for food, and some natural conch pearls are worth a great deal.",
  139: "The largest abalone in the world, with a brick-red shell up to 30 cm long. It grips rocks with a single powerful foot and feeds on drifting kelp. Overharvesting and disease have closed many fisheries for it.",
  140: "A large nudibranch, or sea slug, that swims by flexing its whole red body in waves, like the skirt of a flamenco dancer. It feeds on sponges and lays coiled pink ribbons of eggs. A small shrimp often lives on its back.",
  141: "Has a coiled shell divided into chambers, and adjusts the gas and liquid in them to rise and sink. It migrates up to shallower water at night and back down by day. Its body plan has changed little in hundreds of millions of years.",
  142: "Can change colour and skin texture in a fraction of a second. It is one of the most intelligent invertebrates, able to open jars and escape from tanks. It lives only a year or two and dies soon after breeding.",
  143: "The largest octopus, with an arm span that can reach six metres. It lives in dens among the rocks of the North Pacific and eats crabs, clams and sometimes small sharks. In aquariums, some have learned to recognise individual keepers.",
  144: "One of the most venomous animals in the sea, though it is only the size of a golf ball. Its bite is often painless but carries a nerve toxin that can stop breathing within minutes. The blue rings flash brightly when it is disturbed, a warning to leave it alone.",
  145: "Discovered in Indonesia in 1998, it changes its shape and swimming style to impersonate other animals: a flatfish, a lionfish, a venomous sea snake. It seems to choose which one depending on what is threatening it. It lives on sandy bottoms where there is nowhere to hide.",
  146: "Has two ear-like fins on its head that it flaps to swim, which gave it the name dumbo. It lives deeper than any other octopus, near the seafloor down to several thousand metres. It swallows its prey whole instead of tearing it apart.",
  147: "Controls buoyancy with its cuttlebone, a porous internal shell. It can change colour, pattern and texture to blend in, though it is thought to be colourblind. Before hunting it sometimes ripples bands of dark colour down its body to mesmerise prey.",
  148: "A large, aggressive squid of the eastern Pacific that hunts in groups. It can flash from red to white rapidly, which may be a way of signalling to other squid. Fishermen call it the red devil for its habit of attacking anything in the water.",
  149: "The giant squid is one of the largest invertebrates, and for centuries it was known only from carcasses and sperm whale stomachs. Its eyes, the largest in the animal kingdom, help it spot the faint glow of a whale moving through the dark. It was first filmed alive in its own habitat in 2012.",
  150: "Neither a squid nor an octopus, but the last surviving member of its own order. When threatened it pulls its webbed arms up over its body, turning itself inside out. It eats marine snow, the drifting flakes of dead matter that fall from above.",
  151: "Only about 7 cm long but covered in light organs, it lights up the beaches of Toyama Bay in Japan every spring when it rises to spawn. It can produce light in several colours. It is also a popular food in Japan, often boiled or eaten raw.",
  152: "Lives in rocky burrows along the Atlantic coast of North America. It has two different claws: a heavy one for crushing and a sharper one for tearing. Lobsters keep growing throughout their lives and can live for decades.",
  153: "Has no big claws but long, spiny antennae it uses to defend itself and make a rasping noise. Every autumn, lobsters march across the seabed in long single-file lines, each touching the one in front. It hides in crevices by day.",
  154: "Lives on sandy and muddy bottoms along the Pacific coast of North America. It buries itself in sand to wait for prey, with only its eyes and antennae showing. It is one of the most important commercial crabs on that coast.",
  155: "One of the largest crabs, with a leg span up to 1.8 metres. Young crabs gather in huge piles called pods for protection. It was introduced to the Barents Sea by Soviet scientists, where it has since spread along the Norwegian coast.",
  156: "Lives on cold, muddy bottoms in the North Pacific and North Atlantic, and is fished heavily off Alaska and Canada. Snow crab populations in the Bering Sea crashed sharply around 2021, apparently due to warming water. Females carry their eggs under their abdomen for over a year.",
  157: "Has the longest legs of any arthropod, spanning up to four metres. It lives on the seabed off Japan and can live for a century. It camouflages itself by sticking sponges and other animals to its shell.",
  158: "Its back legs are flattened into paddles, making it a strong swimmer. It is common in bays and estuaries along the Atlantic coast and supports a large fishery in Chesapeake Bay. Its claws are bright blue, and a mature female's tips are red.",
  159: "Punches with its club-like front limbs so quickly that the water around them briefly boils, a strike strong enough to crack aquarium glass. It uses this to smash snails and crabs. Its eyes have up to sixteen kinds of colour receptors, where people have three.",
  160: "Runs cleaning stations where fish come to have parasites and dead skin removed. It often climbs right into the mouths of groupers and moray eels, which hold still for it. It is a popular aquarium shrimp for the same reason.",
  161: "Small, but there may be more krill in the Southern Ocean by weight than any other wild animal on Earth. It forms swarms so dense they turn the water red. Blue whales, penguins, seals and fish all depend on it.",
  162: "A relative of pill bugs and woodlice, but as big as a football. It scavenges on the deep seafloor for dead fish and whales. In captivity one went over four years without eating.",
  163: "Found only in 2005, on hydrothermal vents in the South Pacific. Its arms are covered in long hairs that grow bacteria, which it may farm and eat. It waves its arms in the vent water, perhaps to feed the bacteria with chemicals.",
  164: "Eats coral by turning its stomach inside out over it. Outbreaks of millions of starfish can strip whole reefs, including parts of the Great Barrier Reef. Its long spines carry a toxin that causes painful wounds.",
  165: "A keystone predator on rocky Pacific shores: without it, mussels take over and crowd out other life. That was the experiment that gave ecology the word keystone species. Sea star wasting disease killed many from 2013 onward.",
  166: "One of the largest and fastest sea stars, with up to 24 arms and a span of a metre. It hunts sea urchins, and where it disappears, urchins multiply and strip the kelp. Wasting disease wiped out most of them along the Pacific coast after 2013.",
  167: "Grazes on kelp along the Pacific coast of North America. When predators such as sea otters and sunflower stars disappear, its numbers explode and it can turn kelp forests into bare 'urchin barrens'. It can live for decades.",
  168: "A relative of starfish whose five arms branch over and over into a tangle of tendrils. At night it spreads them into a net in the current to catch plankton. By day it curls up tight into a ball.",
  169: "A large sea cucumber that crawls over sand flats eating sediment and digesting the organic matter in it. When disturbed it throws out sticky white threads that tangle attackers. A small pearlfish sometimes lives inside its body.",
  170: "A deep-sea cucumber that walks across the abyssal seafloor on enlarged tube feet, in groups of hundreds. It eats the organic material in the mud. A small crab often rides on its underside.",
  171: "One of the most common jellyfish, recognised by the four rings visible through its bell. Its sting is too weak to hurt most people. It can shrink and regrow when food is short.",
  172: "The largest known jellyfish, with a bell up to two metres across and tentacles that can trail for more than thirty metres. It prefers cold northern water. Even pieces of tentacle washed ashore can still sting.",
  173: "A golden-brown jelly with long maroon tentacles that drifts along the Pacific coast of North America. It catches plankton and small fish with its stinging tentacles. Blooms of them can clog fishing nets in late summer.",
  174: "Not a jellyfish but a colony of organisms working as one, floating on a gas-filled sail. Its tentacles can trail ten metres or more and sting painfully even when washed up on the beach. The sail is angled left or right, so the wind scatters colonies apart.",
  175: "Possibly the most venomous animal in the sea, with a sting that can kill a person in minutes. It is nearly transparent and has 24 eyes, some with lenses. It actively hunts small fish and shrimp in the shallows of northern Australia.",
  176: "A deep red jellyfish of the deep sea. When attacked, it sets off a ring of spinning blue lights that can be seen from far away, probably calling bigger predators to attack whatever is attacking it. The red colour is invisible at depth, where no red light reaches.",
  177: "Moves by beating rows of cilia that scatter light into rainbows. It was carried to the Black Sea in ships' ballast water in the 1980s and nearly wiped out the anchovy fishery there. It can reproduce when just two weeks old.",
  178: "A colony of thousands of individuals strung together into one long chain, sometimes over forty metres. Each part does a different job: swimming, catching food, digesting or reproducing. It glows blue when disturbed.",
  181: "A worm up to three metres long that buries itself in sand with only its jaws showing. When a fish touches its antennae, it snaps shut with enough force to cut the fish in half and drags it underground. It was named after a famous news story, which says more about biologists than about the worm.",
  188: "No sleeper shark of this size has been confirmed. Fishermen working deep lines off the abyssal plains have reported torn gear and huge, slow shapes on sonar. If it exists, it would be the largest predatory shark alive, living in water close to freezing and hunting by smell.",
  189: "A shy angelfish that rarely ventures far from caves and ledges. Its colour pattern stays much the same from juvenile to adult, unlike most angelfish. It feeds almost entirely on sponges and tunicates, which makes it hard to keep in an aquarium.",
  190: "A large Caribbean angelfish, black with each scale edged in gold. Pairs are thought to mate for life and defend their territory together. Juveniles work as cleaners, picking parasites off larger fish.",
  191: "A small Pacific angelfish of brilliant red-orange with black bars. It lives among rubble and coral heads, rarely far from cover. Like many angelfish, it starts out female and the dominant fish in a group becomes male.",
  192: "A dwarf angelfish of the Indo-Pacific, deep blue blending into orange along its sides. It picks at algae on rock and rubble. It is hardy and one of the most popular angelfish in home aquariums.",
  193: "A large angelfish with sweeping blue lines over a golden-brown body and a blue ring behind its gill cover. Juveniles have white and blue vertical stripes. It feeds on sponges and tunicates along the reef edge.",
  194: "A butterflyfish with a dark mask across its eyes, which earned it the name raccoon. It feeds mostly at night, unlike most of its relatives. It eats coral polyps, worms and small invertebrates.",
  195: "Its long, thin snout works like a pair of tweezers, reaching into cracks for tube feet, fish eggs and tiny shrimp. It has one of the widest ranges of any butterflyfish, from the Red Sea to Mexico. A dark upper half of the head makes its real eye hard to pick out.",
  196: "A common Caribbean butterflyfish that usually travels in pairs. The dark spot near its tail draws attacks away from its head. It feeds on worms and coral polyps picked from the reef.",
  197: "The large ringed spot near its tail is far easier to see than its real eye, which hides inside a dark band. A predator aiming for the head ends up lunging at the tail, and the fish darts off the other way. Pairs stay together for years.",
  198: "Often mistaken for the Moorish idol, but it is a butterflyfish with a long white pennant trailing from its dorsal fin. It gathers in schools above the reef to pick plankton out of the water. Juveniles sometimes act as cleaners.",
  199: "A surgeonfish of the Indian Ocean, powder blue with a black face and white throat. It grazes algae in shallow, surging water near the reef crest. Like all tangs it has a sharp spine on each side of its tail.",
  200: "A tang of Pacific reef crests, where waves break hardest. The orange teardrop around its tail spine is a warning of the blade inside. It is notoriously difficult to keep alive in captivity.",
  201: "A unicornfish without a horn, named for its relatives that have one. The two orange spines by its tail are hooked blades. It feeds mostly on brown algae and is often seen nibbling at kelp-like seaweed on the reef.",
  202: "Named for its stripes, which look like a prison uniform. It forms large grazing schools that overwhelm territorial damselfish by sheer numbers, so they can feed on the algae the damsels defend. It is one of the most common surgeonfish in the Pacific.",
  203: "Can raise its dorsal and anal fins into a tall disc nearly as high as the fish is long, making it look much bigger. Juveniles are striped in yellow and brown. It grazes on algae in lagoons and on reef slopes.",
  204: "Young threespots shelter in sea anemones like clownfish do. Adults lose most of their white spots and live in groups around coral heads. It makes chirping sounds when guarding eggs.",
  205: "A small black-and-white damselfish that lives in groups over a single branching coral, diving into it at any sign of danger. The largest fish in the group is male and keeps a harem of females. It can be surprisingly aggressive toward much bigger fish.",
  206: "Juveniles are dark blue with bright iridescent spots, and look completely different from the adults. It farms its own patch of algae on dead coral and nips at anything that comes near it. Adults lose their spots as they grow.",
  207: "A pale pink clownfish with a white stripe along its back and another behind each eye. It usually lives in magnificent sea anemones. Like other clownfish, a group lives in a strict size order, and only the largest pair breeds.",
  208: "A clownfish that usually lives in bubble-tip anemones. Females are bright red with a single white bar behind the eye, which fades in older fish. It can be very territorial about its anemone.",
  209: "The largest clownfish, with a spine on each cheek that other clownfish lack. Females can be much larger and darker than males. It is among the most aggressive clownfish and will nip at divers who come close to its anemone.",
  210: "A wrasse with a long, curved snout like a bird's beak, used to pick small animals out of the coral. It swims by flapping its pectoral fins like wings. Females are pale and dark-speckled; males are vivid green.",
  211: "A wrasse of Indo-Pacific reefs, green with pink lines and a yellow crescent tail. It is always moving, searching the reef for small crustaceans and fish eggs. Juveniles sometimes clean other fish.",
  212: "Most bluehead wrasse are small and yellow; only the dominant males grow the blue head and black-and-white collar. If the male dies, the largest female changes sex and colour within days. Juveniles often act as cleaners.",
  213: "Purple above and yellow below, and common on Caribbean reefs. Young ones run cleaning stations, picking parasites off larger fish. Adults feed on crabs, brittle stars and urchins.",
  214: "A large wrasse that roots through the sand with its long snout like a pig, looking for clams and crabs. The first three spines of its dorsal fin are long and trailing. All hogfish begin life female and some later change into males.",
  215: "Like many parrotfish, it wraps itself in a bubble of mucus at night. The bubble may hide its scent from moray eels and keep parasites off. Females are brown with a white stripe, very unlike the colourful males.",
  216: "The largest Atlantic parrotfish, with an orange-bronze head and a green body. It depends on mangroves as a nursery and has declined where they have been cleared. It returns to the same cave each night to sleep.",
  217: "One of the largest and most colourful triggerfish of the Atlantic, with blue lines across its face. It blows jets of water at the sand to uncover sea urchins, then flips them to bite the soft underside. It can change colour to match its surroundings.",
  218: "An aggressive triggerfish that bites the spines off sea urchins before cracking them open. It is hostile to other fish and will chase much larger ones away from its patch of reef. Juveniles have green spots and orange lines.",
  219: "Juveniles are bright yellow cubes with black spots and are one of the best-known fish on the reef. As they grow, they turn greyer and their shape stretches out. Like other boxfish, it can release a toxin from its skin when stressed.",
  220: "Has two long horns projecting forward from its head and another pair at the back, like a cow. Its rigid bony box makes it slow, and it relies on the horns and its toxic skin for defence. It blows jets of water at the sand to find small animals.",
  221: "A pufferfish of Indo-Pacific reefs, black with white spots, though some individuals are completely yellow. It feeds on the tips of branching corals. Like other puffers, it carries tetrodotoxin and is deadly if eaten.",
  222: "Its pale body is covered in dark lines that look like streets on a city map. It is one of the largest puffers on the reef. It feeds on sponges, algae and small invertebrates.",
  223: "The adults are spotted, but the juveniles are brown with white blotches and swim with an exaggerated wriggle that imitates a toxic flatworm. That disguise seems to keep predators away. Adults hang in groups under ledges during the day.",
  224: "A snapper that schools tightly by day around coral heads and wrecks, often in hundreds. It was introduced to Hawaii in the 1950s for fishing and spread widely. At night the schools break up to hunt.",
  225: "A common Caribbean snapper, blue-grey with a yellow stripe that runs into its forked tail. It usually swims well above the reef, not close to the bottom. Its flesh is prized in Florida and the Bahamas.",
  226: "An Atlantic snapper with blue lines on its face and a small black spot below its dorsal fin. Adults gather at a few spawning sites each summer. It feeds on crabs, shrimp and fish on reefs and in seagrass.",
  227: "A small grouper covered in blue spots, native to the Indo-Pacific. It was introduced to Hawaii in the 1950s and has since become one of the most common predators on its reefs. It often hunts alongside octopuses and moray eels.",
  228: "A small Atlantic grouper that comes in several colour phases, from red-brown to bright yellow. It shelters in crevices and ambushes small fish and shrimp. Like many groupers it starts life female.",
  229: "Its large eyes give it away as a night feeder. During the day it hides in crevices and under ledges, and comes out after dark to hunt crabs and shrimp. It makes clicking and grunting sounds when disturbed.",
  230: "Schools in dense groups inside reef caves by day, its coppery, nearly transparent body flashing in a diver's light. At night the schools leave the caves to feed on plankton. Its big eyes suit its nocturnal life.",
  231: "A deep red fish with enormous eyes that hunts at night. Red is hard to see in deep water and at night, making it an excellent disguise. It spends the day hiding near the bottom.",
  232: "Juveniles have a very tall, ribbon-like dorsal fin and swim in small circles in the same spot. Adults keep the striking black-and-white pattern but have a shorter fin. It hides under ledges by day and feeds on small crustaceans at night.",
  233: "One of the most common fish on Indo-Pacific reefs, forming clouds of orange and pink over the coral. Each group has a few males and many females. If a male dies, a female changes sex to replace it.",
  234: "A slender goby that hovers above its burrow and flicks its long dorsal spine to signal to others. At any sign of danger it shoots back into the hole. It usually lives in pairs.",
  235: "One of the smallest fish on the reef and a key cleaner in the Caribbean. Groupers and parrotfish wait at its cleaning station with their mouths open while it picks off parasites. It was one of the first marine fish bred in captivity.",
  236: "A small blenny that lives in a hole in the coral, with only its head showing. It darts out to graze on algae and then backs in again. The male changes colour when courting.",
  237: "A blenny with red lips that lives on rocks in the surge zone. It is fiercely territorial and will chase off other algae-eaters. It is easy to overlook until it moves.",
  238: "Digs a burrow in sand and rubble and hovers just above it, picking plankton out of the water. The male carries the eggs in his mouth until they hatch, which means he cannot eat for that time. It retreats into the burrow tail-first.",
  239: "Juveniles drift on their side among mangrove leaves, looking like a dead leaf. Adults are tall and round and gather in small groups at reef edges. It feeds on jellyfish, algae and small invertebrates.",
  240: "A small moray with a pattern of black and yellow blotches on white. It eats mostly crabs and shrimp, crushing them with blunt teeth. It sometimes leaves the water briefly to hunt crabs on rocks.",
  241: "A moray banded in dark brown and white, with pebble-like teeth for crushing crabs and sea urchins. It is generally shy and hides in crevices during the day. It can grow to about 1.5 metres.",
  242: "One of the most common morays in the Caribbean. It hunts at night by smell, poking into crevices for fish and crustaceans. Its spots are dense enough to look like a pale fish with a dark net over it.",
  243: "A long, thin predator that hangs head-down among sea rods and soft corals, swaying with them. It also shadows larger fish, using them as cover to get close to its prey. It sucks fish into its tube-like snout in an instant.",
  244: "A very long, slender fish with a thin whip at the end of its tail. It hunts small fish by sucking them in through its long snout. At night its colour changes to a banded pattern.",
  245: "Lies motionless on the reef, covered in flaps and mottling that match the rocks around it. When threatened, it flashes the inside of its pectoral fins, which are bright black and white. Its dorsal spines are venomous.",
  246: "Sways back and forth like a leaf drifting on the reef. It sheds its outer skin every few weeks, possibly to stay free of algae and parasites. Colours range from white and yellow to red and brown.",
  247: "A small, lumpy frogfish with bright warts and patches, often found on sponges that it closely matches. It walks across the reef on its pectoral fins and fishes with a lure on its head. It can change colour over a few weeks.",
  248: "Rests in caves and under ledges during the day, often in groups. At night it hunts fish and octopuses hiding in crevices, wriggling into gaps other sharks cannot reach. It can pump water over its gills while resting.",
  249: "Patrols the drop-offs of Indo-Pacific reefs in groups. When threatened, it arches its back, raises its snout and drops its pectoral fins in a warning display. Divers who ignore it risk a bite.",
  250: "The most common shark on Caribbean reefs. It sometimes rests motionless in caves, a habit once thought impossible for this kind of shark. It eats reef fish and rays.",
  251: "Juveniles are dark with white stripes, while adults are tan with dark spots, so the name only fits the young. It rests on the sand by day and hunts snails and crabs in crevices at night. Females in captivity have produced young without a male.",
  252: "A flat carpet shark fringed with branching flaps of skin around its head. It lies on the bottom under ledges, looking like part of the reef, and waits for fish to swim close. It can swallow a fish nearly as large as itself.",
  253: "A small shark that walks over reef flats on its fins. It can survive for hours in very low-oxygen water in tide pools by shutting down parts of its brain. The large dark spots behind its pectoral fins look like the epaulettes on a uniform.",
  254: "Lives in shallow water among mangroves and seagrass. Females return to the bay where they were born to give birth. It is one of the best-studied sharks, partly because it does well in captivity.",
  255: "Gulps air at the surface and holds it in its stomach, which lets it hover motionless in the water. Its ragged teeth are always on show. In the womb, the first embryos to hatch eat their smaller siblings.",
  256: "Can live in fresh water and has been found far up the Mississippi and the Amazon. It is stocky and short-snouted and hunts in murky, shallow water. It is considered one of the most dangerous sharks to people.",
  257: "The smaller of the two manta species, living around coastlines and reefs. It visits cleaning stations, where small fish pick parasites from its skin. Each one has a unique pattern of spots on its belly.",
  258: "A strange mix of shark and ray, with a broad head and rows of thorny ridges over its back. It lives on sandy and muddy bottoms near reefs and eats crabs and shellfish. It is critically endangered after heavy fishing.",
  259: "Can produce shocks of over 200 volts from electric organs in its wings. It ambushes fish from the sand, wraps its body around them and stuns them. Ancient Greeks and Romans used it to numb pain.",
  260: "A small stingray whose net-like pattern can shift to match the sand it lies on. It buries itself and waits for small fish and crustaceans. Its tail spine is venomous.",
  261: "A tuna with very long pectoral fins, sold as white-meat tuna. It migrates across the North Pacific in schools. Young albacore stay near the surface and dive deeper as they grow.",
  262: "A large tuna with big eyes that dives several hundred metres by day to hunt squid and fish in cold water. Its fatty meat is prized for sashimi. It keeps its body warm with a special network of blood vessels.",
  263: "A sleek, fast jack with blue and yellow stripes along its sides. It forms schools in open water and around reefs. It often follows larger fish and sharks.",
  264: "The largest jack, with an amber stripe through its eye. It gathers around wrecks and oil rigs. Large ones have been linked to ciguatera poisoning.",
  265: "Swims alongside sharks, rays and turtles, taking scraps and gaining protection. Sharks rarely eat it. It was once believed to lead ships to land.",
  266: "Has a sucker disc on top of its head, a modified fin, that lets it ride on sharks, whales and turtles. It feeds on scraps and parasites. Some fishermen once tied remoras to lines to catch turtles.",
  267: "A slim fish with a beak-like jaw that skips across the surface in schools to escape predators. It is a popular autumn food in Japan, where it is grilled whole. It migrates across the North Pacific.",
  268: "A needle-jawed fish that hunts small fish at the surface and can leap clear of the water. It has injured people by jumping into boats at night, attracted to lights. Its bones are green.",
  269: "Its stripes light up electric blue when it attacks a school of fish. It slashes through the school with its bill and then eats the stunned fish. It is popular with sport fishermen.",
  270: "One of the largest and fastest billfish, reaching over 700 kg. Its pectoral fins are stiff and cannot fold against its body. It hunts tuna and squid in warm open water.",
  271: "A long, fang-toothed predator with a tall sail-like dorsal fin. It will eat almost anything, including others of its own kind. Its stomach contents have given scientists new species of deep-sea animals.",
  272: "An oily fish of deep water that rises to hunt at night. Its flesh contains wax esters that the body cannot digest, and eating too much causes digestive trouble. It is sometimes mislabelled as white tuna.",
  273: "A slim shark with skin so finely scaled it feels smooth to the touch. It follows schools of tuna across the open ocean. It is one of the most heavily fished sharks for the fin trade.",
  274: "Once one of the most common large animals on Earth, it has declined by more than 90 percent in some oceans. It is bold and curious and was known to approach shipwreck survivors. Its long, rounded fins are tipped white.",
  275: "Uses its long upper tail like a whip, slapping it over its head to stun fish. Its tail can be as long as its body. It sometimes leaps clear of the water.",
  276: "Has huge eyes that look upward through keyhole-shaped sockets. It dives deep by day and rises at night, following its prey. Like the common thresher, it uses its long tail to stun fish.",
  277: "A rare mako with long pectoral fins, which may help it glide through deeper water. Very little is known about how it lives. It is often caught by accident on tuna longlines.",
  278: "A ray related to mantas that dives to hundreds of metres. It leaps high out of the water, sometimes in groups, for reasons not fully understood. It feeds on krill and small fish.",
  279: "The only stingray that lives in the open ocean rather than on the bottom. It swims by flapping its wings like a manta. It follows squid and jellyfish up toward the surface at night.",
  285: "A brown-spotted cod of the North Pacific with a chin barbel for feeling the bottom. It feeds on crabs, shrimp and fish near the seafloor. It supports a large fishery in Alaska.",
  286: "A dark green-grey relative of cod, also called coley or pollock. It forms large schools in the North Atlantic. Young saithe live close to shore before moving offshore.",
  287: "A long, eel-like member of the cod family that hides among rocks and wrecks. It can grow over two metres. It feeds on fish and crabs.",
  288: "A silver-grey predator with a mouth full of sharp teeth. It stays near the bottom by day and rises in the water at night to hunt. It is one of the most important fish for Spanish and Portuguese markets.",
  289: "A round, knobbly fish that clings to rocks with a sucker on its belly. Males guard the eggs and fan them with their fins. Its eggs are sold as a cheap substitute for caviar.",
  290: "A salmon relative that lives farther north than any other freshwater fish. Some populations migrate to sea and back. Its belly turns bright red at spawning time.",
  291: "The smallest and most common Pacific salmon. Spawning males grow a pronounced hump on their backs. It has a strict two-year life cycle.",
  292: "A silver salmon that turns red with a hooked jaw before it spawns. Young coho spend a year or more in streams before going to sea. It is popular with anglers for its fight.",
  293: "A rainbow trout that migrates to sea and returns to rivers to spawn. Unlike most Pacific salmon, it can spawn several times. Its pink stripe becomes brighter in fresh water.",
  294: "A small fish that spawns on beaches in huge numbers, sometimes washing ashore in silver heaps. It is a key food for cod, seabirds and whales. Male capelin have a ridge of scales along their sides.",
  295: "A jack with a row of bony plates along its side. It forms large schools in the eastern Atlantic. It is a common food fish in Europe.",
  296: "A silver predator of rocky shores and surf. It is popular with anglers and is farmed widely in the Mediterranean. It can live for over twenty years.",
  297: "A dark, chunky bass of the western Atlantic. Breeding males grow a blue hump on their heads. Like many sea bass, it starts life female.",
  298: "A large sculpin with a huge head and mottled skin that matches the rocks it rests on. Its eggs are poisonous to people. It eats crabs, small fish and molluscs.",
  299: "Males and females look so different they were once thought to be separate species. Males are grey with blue spots, females brown with orange freckles. It lives in kelp forests along the Pacific coast.",
  300: "Spends its whole adult life around a single crevice on rocky Pacific reefs. Its black body with a yellow stripe makes it easy to spot. It can live for over 75 years.",
  301: "A bright red rockfish of deep rocky reefs from Alaska to Mexico. Like all rockfish it gives birth to live larvae, sometimes hundreds of thousands at once. Brought up quickly from depth, its swim bladder swells and pushes its eyes out, which is why anglers now lower them back down on weighted hooks.",
  302: "Schools in the kelp forest by day, feeding on plankton and jellyfish. Its colour varies from blue-grey to almost black. It is one of the most common rockfish in shallow water.",
  303: "A bass of the California kelp forest with a checkerboard pattern on its back. It hunts small fish and crabs among the kelp stalks. It can live for over thirty years.",
  304: "An olive-green fish with bright blue eyes, common in tide pools and kelp along the California coast. It grazes on algae and can survive out of water briefly. Juveniles live in tide pools.",
  305: "Males hum through the night to call females to their nests under rocks, a drone loud enough that houseboat owners in Sausalito once blamed it on secret military tests. The female leaves the eggs and the male guards them alone. The rows of lights along its belly look like the buttons on a sailor's uniform, which is where the name comes from.",
  306: "Spends summer in deeper, cooler water and moves into shallow bays to spawn in the middle of winter. Unusually for a fish, it lays eggs that sink and stick to the bottom. Proteins in its blood work as antifreeze, so it can sit in water just above freezing.",
  307: "Lies buried in sand with only its eyes showing, changing its colour to match the bottom within minutes. Unlike most flatfish it chases its prey off the bottom, sometimes all the way to the surface. Large females are called doormats by anglers.",
  308: "Feeds at night on worms and small clams, finding them by touch and smell with the small hair-like growths on the underside of its head. It spends the day buried in sand or mud. The fillets are what restaurants mean by Dover sole.",
  309: "A flatfish with black and orange bands across its fins, found in the North Pacific. It can be right-eyed or left-eyed. It often swims into rivers.",
  310: "A skate of European waters with thorny spines along its back and tail. Its egg cases, called mermaid's purses, often wash up on beaches. It feeds on crabs and small fish on the bottom.",
  311: "The largest skate on the Pacific coast of North America. Each wing has a dark eyespot. Its egg cases can hold several embryos.",
  312: "A small grey shark with a venomous spine in front of each dorsal fin. It can live for over a hundred years, and its pregnancy lasts nearly two years. It schools in thousands and was once heavily fished.",
  313: "A warm-bodied shark of the North Pacific that hunts salmon in icy water. It can keep its body temperature over 15 degrees warmer than the sea. It is related to the great white.",
  314: "A stocky shark of cold seas, related to the great white. It has been seen playing with floating kelp, one of the few fish known to play. It is now endangered after heavy fishing.",
  315: "A slow, huge-mouthed shark of the cold North Pacific. It scavenges dead animals and hunts slow prey such as giant octopuses. It can grow over six metres.",
  316: "An ancient shark with seven gill slits instead of five. It hunts seals and other sharks, sometimes in packs. It often comes into shallow bays.",
  317: "A grey shark with dark saddles along its back, common in California bays. Females gather in warm, shallow water in summer, possibly to speed up the development of their young. It eats crabs, clams and small fish.",
  318: "An eagle ray with a raised head, common along the California coast. It digs clams and worms out of the mud by flapping its wings. Its tail has a venomous spine.",
  319: "A viperfish of the North Pacific, smaller and paler than Sloane's, with the same needle-like fangs that do not fit inside its mouth. A light organ on the first ray of its dorsal fin works as a lure. At night it rises hundreds of metres to hunt, and by dawn it is back in the dark.",
  320: "Produces red light from organs below its eyes, which most deep-sea animals cannot see. It uses this light like a sniper scope to spot prey that does not know it is there. Its jaw has no floor, which may help it close its mouth quickly.",
  321: "A long, slender dragonfish covered in a pattern of hexagonal scales, each with a dark outline. Its chin barbel ends in a glowing tip it can twitch to lure prey. Its stomach is lined black so that the glowing animals it eats do not light it up from inside.",
  322: "Females are long, black and fanged, while males are tiny, brown and toothless. The larvae have eyes on long stalks. It is a close relative of the Atlantic black dragonfish.",
  323: "An anglerfish with three glowing warts beside its lure. Males are tiny and fuse permanently to the female, becoming little more than sperm-producing attachments. It is one of the most common deep-sea anglerfish.",
  324: "A round, warty anglerfish the size of a football, with a long lure that branches into fleshy threads at the tip. Females are the large ones; males are tiny, free-swimming and live just long enough to find a female. The first specimens came from Greenland in the 1830s.",
  325: "Instead of a glowing lure, it relies on long, hair-like fin rays all over its body that pick up the faintest movement in the water. Prey that brushes against them gets swallowed. As in many anglers, the males are tiny and fuse to the females for life.",
  326: "Its lure dangles from a whip longer than its entire body, which it may hold out in front as it drifts. It has been filmed swimming upside down near the seafloor, trailing the lure just above the mud. Males are tiny and live free.",
  327: "Perhaps the most numerous vertebrate on Earth, with hundreds of trillions alive at any time. It is only a few centimetres long and lives in the twilight zone. Few people have ever heard of it.",
  328: "A ribbon-like fish with telescope eyes that point upward. Its mouth can expand to forty times its normal size to suck in tiny prey. It hangs vertically in the water.",
  329: "A thread-thin eel whose bird-like jaws curve apart and never close. It may catch shrimp by tangling their legs in its tiny teeth. Its body can have more than 750 vertebrae.",
  330: "An eel-shaped fish that feeds head-down on the seabed, nibbling at sea anemones and other soft animals. A row of short spines runs along its back. Its larvae are huge for a fish larva and drift for a long time before settling.",
  331: "A flat-headed ambush predator that lies motionless on the deep seafloor, its mouth lined with rows of hooked teeth. It is one of the top predators of the deep slope. Each one is both male and female.",
  332: "One of the deepest fish ever caught, hauled up from more than 8,000 metres in the Puerto Rico Trench in 1970. It is small, pale and soft, with a weak skeleton suited to enormous pressure. Almost nothing else is known about it.",
  333: "A rattail with big eyes and a pointed snout, found along the deep slopes of the North Atlantic. It can live for seventy years and was fished hard from the 1970s, after shallower stocks ran out. It feeds on shrimp and small squid near the bottom.",
  334: "A common rattail of the deep Pacific slope, gathering at anything dead that sinks to the bottom. It also hunts squid and small fish. Like its relatives, it grows slowly and can live for many decades.",
  335: "A long, ribbon-like black fish with large eyes and a mouth full of long teeth. On the island of Madeira it is caught on lines set at night and is a local favourite, served with banana. It rises toward the surface after dark to hunt.",
  336: "Its tube-shaped eyes point forward, giving it binocular vision, which is rare in the deep. Its stomach can stretch to hold prey larger than itself. Young telescopefish look so different from adults that they were once thought to be a separate family.",
  337: "A flat, deep-bodied relative of the John Dory that lives around seamounts in the southern oceans. It grows very slowly and can pass a hundred years of age. Fishermen working deep trawls off New Zealand and Tasmania bring it up in dense schools.",
  338: "A soft, pale fish of the deep slope, with a head bare of scales and big dark eyes. It eats jellyfish and other gelatinous animals that few other fish want. Trawls on the deep slope of the North Atlantic often bring up large numbers of them.",
  339: "A rare relative of the sand tiger, larger and darker, with spotted sides and the same ragged teeth. Most sightings come from deep reef walls, seamounts and steep drop-offs. Divers who have met one describe it as calm and curious.",
  340: "One of the smallest sharks, barely as long as a forearm. Its belly glows to match the faint light from above and hide its outline, and light organs along its spines may warn predators that it is not worth the trouble. It lives on the deep shelves of the eastern Atlantic.",
  341: "The largest vertebrate known to glow, reaching nearly two metres; it was only confirmed in 2021. Its lower teeth form a single saw-edged blade, used to cut chunks out of fish larger than itself. A huge oily liver keeps it near neutral buoyancy.",
  342: "One of the deepest-living sharks, filmed alive at more than 3,500 metres. Its large, oil-rich liver keeps it afloat without a swim bladder. That same liver oil is the reason it is fished.",
  343: "A sluggish deep-water shark covered in small thorn-like scales. It hunts by moving slowly up to prey and sucking it in by expanding its throat. Groups of them rest at the bottom of submarine canyons, sometimes in shallow water near Monterey.",
  344: "An octopus that hunts by day, moving from crevice to crevice across Indo-Pacific reefs. It changes colour and texture constantly as it travels, sometimes flashing dark rings to unsettle prey. Groupers and goatfish often follow it, snatching whatever it flushes out.",
  345: "Carries coconut shells and clam halves across the seabed and pulls them together around itself when threatened. It walks on two arms while hauling the shells, an awkward-looking stilt walk. This was one of the first observations of tool use in an invertebrate.",
  346: "Its pattern of rust-brown and white bands is unique to each animal, so divers and scientists can identify individuals from photographs. It hunts over sand and rubble by spreading its arms like a net. It was only formally described in 2006.",
  347: "One of the largest octopuses known, possibly the heaviest, despite its gelatinous body. Males keep one arm curled in a sac under the right eye, which is why it looks seven-armed. Deep-sea cameras have filmed it holding jellyfish against its body, possibly as food or a shield.",
  348: "Almost completely transparent, with only its eyes, gut and digestive gland showing. Its eyes are long and narrow rather than round, which shrinks the shadow they cast from below. It lives in tropical twilight waters and is rarely seen alive.",
  349: "A small, squashed octopus that lives on the deep seafloor and spreads its webbed arms to sit flat like a pancake. It flaps two small fins to swim. It probably feeds on worms and small crustaceans picked off the mud.",
  350: "The heaviest invertebrate known, up to about 500 kg. Its arms carry swivelling hooks, and its eyes are the largest of any animal ever measured, about the size of a dinner plate. Only a handful have ever been seen intact, most of them caught by toothfish boats in the Antarctic.",
  351: "Known only from a few dozen videos, mostly shot by deep-sea robots and drilling rigs. Its arms bend sharply at an elbow and trail metres below the body, and may be used to catch passing animals. No adult has ever been caught.",
  352: "Has one large eye that looks upward for silhouettes against the faint light above, and one small eye that looks down for glowing animals below. Its skin is covered in light organs and red pigment. Young ones have two eyes of the same size.",
  353: "A large squid with a diamond-shaped fin running the length of its body, usually found in pairs. It lays its eggs in a long gelatinous tube that drifts near the surface. In Japan it is fished at night with lights.",
  354: "Lives in small schools over Caribbean reefs and seagrass and talks with colour, flashing patterns at one another. A male courting a female can show one display on the side facing her and a warning to rivals on the other. It feeds on small fish and shrimp.",
  355: "A glassy, nearly transparent squid that pulls its head and arms into its body when threatened and fills itself with water until it is round. Its body fluid is lighter than seawater, which keeps it afloat. It drifts through the twilight zone of the open ocean.",
  356: "Keeps glowing bacteria in a special organ under its body and uses their light to cancel its own shadow on moonlit nights. Each morning it vents most of the bacteria and lets the population regrow by evening. It spends the day buried in sand off Hawaii.",
  357: "Walks along the seabed on its arms rather than swimming, flashing bands of purple, yellow and red. Its flesh is toxic, which may be why it can afford to be so conspicuous. It hunts small fish and crustaceans on sandy and muddy bottoms.",
  358: "One of the largest cuttlefish, found on coral reefs from the Indian Ocean to Fiji. It hypnotises prey with dark bands that ripple down its body before striking with its tentacles. Females lay eggs deep inside branching coral.",
  359: "Has flat, shovel-shaped plates in place of the long antennae of other lobsters. It hides in caves and under ledges in the Mediterranean and comes out at night to feed on shellfish. It has become rare after years of overfishing.",
  360: "A small, flattened relative of the hermit crab, red with blue lines, that hides under rocks and in crevices. Its long claws are covered in spines. It is common on rocky coasts around Europe but rarely noticed.",
  361: "Buries itself backwards in sand with only its eyes and antennae showing, waiting for small fish and worms. Its flattened claws, used to dig, look a little like spanners. It is caught commercially off Queensland and Hawaii.",
  362: "A heavy-clawed crab of the Pacific coast of North America, brick-red on top with black-tipped claws. Its claws are strong enough to crack the shells of snails and barnacles. It lives in rocky areas and bays and is often caught off docks.",
  363: "A large crab of European coasts with a shell edged like a pie crust and powerful black-tipped claws. It moves offshore in winter and can travel hundreds of kilometres along the coast. It is one of the most important shellfish caught in Britain.",
  364: "Has red eyes, a velvety coat of short hairs and back legs flattened into paddles. It is known for standing its ground and raising its claws at anything that comes close. It is fished in France and Spain.",
  365: "A small hermit crab of Indo-Pacific reefs with legs banded in orange and red. It lives in borrowed cone shells and moves house as it grows. It scavenges dead animals and algae.",
  366: "The largest mantis shrimp, up to 40 cm. It waits in a burrow in the sand with only its eyes showing and spears passing fish with its barbed front limbs. Fishermen who handle it carelessly call it the thumb splitter.",
  367: "Snaps one oversized claw shut so fast that it shoots a jet of water and forms a collapsing bubble, which briefly reaches thousands of degrees and stuns prey. Colonies of snapping shrimp make so much noise that they can interfere with submarine sonar. Many live in pairs with gobies, sharing a burrow.",
  368: "Eats nothing but starfish. A pair will flip a starfish onto its back, pin it, and feed on it over several days, sometimes keeping it alive by feeding on the arms first. It lives in pairs on Indo-Pacific reefs.",
  369: "A small, see-through shrimp striped in red that cleans parasites from fish. In home aquariums it is valued for eating Aiptasia, a small anemone that spreads like a weed. It lives in the Caribbean and the Gulf of Mexico.",
  370: "A deep-water shrimp that defends itself by spewing a cloud of glowing liquid at an attacker, which hides its escape. Its red colour makes it invisible in the deep, where red light does not reach. It migrates toward the surface at night.",
  371: "Lives in the North Atlantic and rises toward the surface every night to feed. Its light organs flash blue, possibly to keep the swarm together. It is a main food of fin whales and many fish.",
  372: "A tan sea star studded with dark knobs that look like chocolate chips. It lives in shallow sandy lagoons and seagrass beds in the Indo-Pacific. It is often collected and sold dried as a souvenir.",
  373: "A bright blue sea star with smooth, round arms. It can regrow an entire body from a single broken arm. It lives on shallow reefs in the Indo-Pacific.",
  374: "A sea star so puffed up it barely has arms, looking more like a pentagonal cushion. It feeds on coral polyps by turning its stomach out over them. Small pearlfish sometimes live inside its body.",
  375: "A common sea star of the Pacific coast, with short webbed arms and colours ranging from orange to purple. It scavenges dead and decaying matter on the floor of the kelp forest. It can have more than five arms.",
  376: "A brittle star with long spiny arms that forms dense beds on the seabed, sometimes thousands per square metre. It feeds by holding its arms up in the current to catch drifting particles. It is common around Europe.",
  377: "A crinoid, an animal related to sea stars that perches on coral and spreads its feathery arms into the current to catch plankton. It can swim by waving its arms. Its ancestors date back hundreds of millions of years.",
  378: "A black sea urchin with needle-like spines up to 30 cm long. The spines are brittle and break off in skin, causing painful wounds. It grazes on algae at night and hides in crevices by day.",
  379: "Has thick red spines like crayons and wedges itself into reef crevices. It grazes on algae. Its spines are used to make wind chimes.",
  380: "One of the longest-lived animals known, with some individuals over 200 years old. It grazes on kelp along the Pacific coast and is fished for its roe, sold as uni. It has long red spines.",
  381: "A flattened sea urchin that lives half-buried in sandy bottoms. It feeds on tiny particles caught between the sand grains. Its bleached skeleton is often found on beaches.",
  382: "A purple sea cucumber with bright feeding tentacles that it waves in the current. It can release a toxin when stressed. It is popular but risky in home aquariums.",
  383: "A pink, see-through sea cucumber that can swim above the deep seafloor. It sheds its glowing skin to escape predators. It lives in deep oceans around the world.",
  384: "A reddish sea cucumber covered in soft spikes that grazes the seabed. It can expel its internal organs to distract predators and regrow them. It is fished in the Pacific Northwest.",
  385: "A giant sea snail that hunts crown-of-thorns starfish and other sea stars. Its shell has been used as a trumpet in many cultures. It is now rare due to shell collecting.",
  386: "A glossy, leopard-spotted shell that the animal covers with its mantle at night. It is one of the most popular shells for collectors. It grazes on algae on the reef.",
  387: "A tiny snail with an orange-spotted mantle that grazes on sea fans. It stores toxins from the sea fans in its body. Its shell is plain; the colour is all in its soft mantle.",
  388: "The most venomous cone snail, which harpoons fish with a toxic dart. Its venom can kill a human. It hunts small fish by engulfing them with its mouth.",
  389: "A huge fan-shaped clam that stands upright in seagrass meadows. It can grow over a metre long. Most populations in the Mediterranean were wiped out by a parasite after 2016.",
  390: "A burrowing clam with a long neck-like siphon far too big for its shell. It can live for over 160 years. It is fished in the Pacific Northwest and is a delicacy in China.",
  391: "Has dozens of small blue eyes along its mantle. It swims by clapping its shells together. It lives in seagrass beds along the east coast of North America.",
  392: "The most widely farmed oyster in the world, native to the Pacific coast of Asia. It grows fast and tolerates a wide range of conditions. It has become invasive in some places.",
  393: "A small blue sea slug that floats upside down at the surface, held up by a bubble of air in its stomach. It eats Portuguese man o' war and stores their stinging cells in its own body. Its sting can be worse than the man o' war's.",
  394: "A ruffled green sea slug that keeps chloroplasts from the algae it eats alive in its body. It can use them to make food from sunlight for weeks. It lives in the Caribbean.",
  395: "A pale blue nudibranch with black lines and white-edged gills, found on Indo-Pacific reefs. It feeds on sponges. Its colours warn predators that it tastes bad.",
  396: "A tiny, fluffy nudibranch whose sensory horns look like rabbit ears. The fluff is made of small rods on its skin. It feeds on sponges and is toxic.",
  397: "A tiny, translucent sea slug that swims by flapping wing-like lobes. It hunts sea butterflies, another kind of swimming snail. It lives in cold waters of the Arctic and Antarctic.",
  398: "Lies bell-down on the seabed in shallow lagoons, with algae living in its frilly arms. It pulses its bell to circulate water over the algae, which make food from sunlight. It can release stinging mucus into the water.",
  399: "Looks exactly like a fried egg floating in the water. It lives in the Mediterranean and gathers in large numbers in late summer. Its sting is mild and harmless to people.",
  400: "A heavy, rubbery jellyfish whose bell can reach the size of a dustbin lid. It has no long tentacles; it catches plankton through thousands of tiny mouths on its frilly arms. Its sting is mild, and young fish often shelter among those arms.",
  401: "Glows when it is disturbed, so a ship passing through a swarm at night leaves a trail of light. Unlike most jellyfish it skips the polyp stage, which lets its blooms appear in open water almost overnight. Swarms of it have wiped out salmon farms in Ireland.",
  402: "Pale cream with brown V-shapes radiating from the top of its bell, like the points of a compass. It starts life male and becomes female as it grows. It washes up on British beaches in late summer, and its sting is painful.",
  403: "Its rim glows green thanks to a protein that has become one of the most useful tools in biology. Scientists attach that protein to other genes to watch them working inside living cells, work that won a Nobel Prize in 2008. In the wild the jelly is almost invisible until it lights.",
  404: "One of the largest jellyfish known, with a dark purple bell nearly a metre wide and tentacles that trail several metres. For most of the twentieth century it was known only from a few sightings. Then, in 1999, thousands turned up off San Diego.",
  405: "A box jellyfish about the size of a fingernail, nearly invisible in the water. Its sting barely registers at first, but twenty minutes later it can bring on crushing pain, vomiting and a dangerous rise in blood pressure. It has killed swimmers in northern Australia.",
  406: "A huge, thin, almost transparent bell that billows like a sheet in the water. It seems to catch prey by wrapping the whole bell around it like a bag. Only a few have ever been filmed, all by deep-sea vehicles.",
  407: "A deep red jelly that avoids light so strongly that it sinks deeper in the day and only rises in darkness. When touched it glows blue. In some Norwegian fjords it has become so common that it fills the water column.",
  408: "Named for its blood-red stomach, which hides the glow of the luminous animals it has eaten. Without that, its own meal would light it up for predators. It was only described in 2001, from deep-sea robot footage.",
  409: "A tiny, round comb jelly about the size of a grape, with two long sticky tentacles it trails like fishing lines. It reels them in once they have caught a small animal. Rows of beating cilia scatter light into rainbows along its body.",
  410: "A comb jelly drawn out into a flat ribbon over a metre long. It swims by rippling its body and catches plankton along its edges. In sunlight its body shimmers in violet and blue.",
  411: "A barrel-shaped animal that pumps water through its body, filtering out plankton as it goes. Salps alternate between living alone and linking into chains that can stretch for metres. Despite looking like jellyfish, they are closer relatives of vertebrates.",
  412: "A hollow tube made of thousands of tiny clones, each pumping water through the tube to move the colony. It glows brightly when touched, and the light can pass from one end to the other. Large pyrosome blooms have clogged fishing nets along the Pacific coast.",
  413: "A blue disc topped by a small stiff sail that catches the wind and blows it across the sea surface. Huge fleets of them wash up on beaches in spring. Some colonies have their sails angled one way and some the other, so the wind splits them up.",
  414: "A small round float, deep blue, fringed with stinging tentacles. It drifts at the surface with other blue animals such as the by-the-wind sailor and the blue dragon. Its sting is mild for people.",
  434: "A fireworm covered in white bristles that break off in skin and inject a burning toxin. It feeds on coral, anemones and fireworm-sized prey on Caribbean reefs. It can regrow most of its body if broken in two.",
  437: "A fat worm covered in a coat of hair-like bristles that make it look like a mouse. The bristles act like photonic crystals and flash iridescent green and red. It burrows in mud and sand in the North Atlantic.",
  438: "Builds a house of mucus up to a metre across that it uses to filter food from the water. When the filters clog, it abandons the house and builds another, and the old ones sink, carrying carbon to the deep. The animal itself is only about the size of a finger.",
  440: "Has spindly legs that can span seventy centimetres, and a body so thin that its gut runs down into its legs. It sucks its food up through a proboscis. It lives on the deep seafloor in many oceans.",
  441: "Covered in sharp spines, with a long snout for sucking up small shrimp. It lives on deeper reefs and rubble slopes across the Indo-Pacific, often clinging to sponges and sea fans. Like every seahorse, it is the male who carries the eggs and gives birth.",
  442: "A short-nosed seahorse of European coasts that wraps its tail around seagrass and waits for small shrimp to drift past. Its numbers have fallen wherever seagrass meadows have been dredged or anchored over. Pairs greet each other every morning with a short dance.",
  443: "The largest seahorse on the Pacific coast of the Americas, ranging from California to Peru. Many end up as bycatch in shrimp trawls and are dried and sold for traditional medicine. A male can give birth to more than a thousand young at once.",
  444: "A relative of seahorses with lace-like fins and a coat of tiny frills, drifting head-down beside sea fans and feather stars. Here it is the female who carries the eggs, in a pouch formed by her fused belly fins. It changes colour to match whatever it is hiding among.",
  445: "Swims head-down in tight schools that turn as one, often hovering among the spines of sea urchins for cover. Its body is sealed in thin bony plates that come to a sharp edge along its belly, like a razor. It feeds on tiny plankton picked from the water.",
  446: "A flattened, leaf-green pipefish that lies among seagrass blades and is almost impossible to see. It swims slowly by waving its dorsal fin, with its tail curled to hold on. Males carry the eggs on a patch under the body.",
  447: "A dragonfish whose chin barbel ends in a glowing bulb that it may wave as a lure. Under its eyes it has organs that make red light, letting it see prey that cannot see that colour at all. It hunts small fish and shrimp in the dark middle waters.",
  448: "The only backboned animal known to focus light with a mirror rather than a lens. Each eye is split in two: one half looks up through a normal lens, the other looks down using a curved mirror made of tiny crystal plates. It was only found to work this way in 2009.",
  449: "A larger relative of the silver hatchetfish, thin as a blade with mirrored sides. Rows of lights along its belly match the faint daylight from above, so a predator looking up sees nothing. It spends the day several hundred metres down and rises at night.",
  450: "A small lanternfish of the cold North Atlantic that rises toward the surface every night in vast numbers, then sinks before dawn. Cod, herring and seabirds all depend on it. The pattern of light organs on its body is used to tell it from its many relatives.",
  451: "A huge-headed, soft-bodied sculpin of the deep North Pacific, a close cousin of the blobfish. Females lay their eggs in nests on the seabed and the adults stay to guard them, which is unusual so deep. Its loose, watery flesh lets it hover just above the mud without effort.",
  452: "A small anglerfish whose lure ends in three bony hooks, like a fishing tackle. Its upper jaw is hinged so that it can swing down over prey, closing like a trap. It was named in 1925 from a specimen caught off Madeira.",
  453: "A pale, nearly colourless anglerfish that has no lure at all, unlike most of its relatives. Males are tiny and fuse to the female's body for life, sharing her blood supply. Its skin is so thin that its organs can be seen through it.",
  454: "A round, pink anglerfish that walks over the seabed on its pectoral and pelvic fins. When threatened it gulps water and swells up. Its small lure sits folded in a groove on its snout until it needs it.",
  455: "A flat, round fish that crawls over the seabed on its arm-like fins rather than swimming. It lures small crabs and worms with a short fleshy bait above its mouth. It lives in the Gulf of Mexico and the western Atlantic.",
  456: "A long, scaleless predator with dagger-like fangs and no swim bladder. It hunts in the twilight and is a known predator of young salmon at sea. Its fragile body is rarely brought up in one piece.",
  457: "Has tube-shaped eyes that point upward and fangs in its lower jaw that slide into sheaths in its head when it closes its mouth. It hunts small fish and squid against the faint light from above. Young ones look so different that they were once given a different name.",
  458: "Its upward-pointing tube eyes carry a pearly patch on the side that catches light coming from other angles. That lets it watch the water around it as well as above. It hunts small fish in the twilight zone of all the world's oceans.",
  459: "A pale, eel-like fish with a long snout that it pushes through the soft mud of the deep slope, hunting worms and small crustaceans. Despite its shape, it is not a true eel, though the two are related. It swims slowly near the bottom.",
  460: "A grey eel of the deep slope that is often the first animal to reach a carcass on the seabed, sometimes within minutes. It has sharp teeth and tears off pieces by spinning its body. It is common across the deep North Atlantic.",
  461: "A small black eel shaped like an arrow, with long beak-like jaws and a short tail. Its larvae are tall and leaf-shaped like those of other eels. Almost nothing is known about how it lives.",
  462: "A ghost shark with a long, blade-like snout packed with sensors that pick up the electric fields of buried prey. It has a venomous spine in front of its dorsal fin. It lives on the deep slopes of the Atlantic.",
  463: "A shark no longer than a hand, with a pocket beside each pectoral fin that releases a glowing fluid, possibly to confuse predators. Only two have ever been found, one in the eastern Pacific in 1979 and one in the Gulf of Mexico in 2010. The second was not recognised as anything new until years later.",
  464: "A jet-black lanternshark with a faint glow and big green eyes, described in 2015. It was named after Peter Benchley, author of Jaws, who spent his later life campaigning for shark conservation. The scientists who named it took the 'ninja' part from a suggestion by children.",
  465: "Its jaws are slung on stretchy ligaments, so it can throw them forward out of its head to grab prey and then fold them back. It has long, curved teeth like a viper's fangs. It rises toward the surface at night to hunt.",
  466: "A large, soft, sluggish shark of the deep, with a long low first dorsal fin that runs along much of its back. It has a huge oily liver and may drift more than it swims. Embryos feed on unfertilised eggs in the womb.",
  467: "A grey dogfish of the deep slope with huge, glowing green eyes that gather what little light there is. It grows slowly and has very few young, so heavy fishing for its liver oil cut its numbers sharply. It feeds on fish and squid near the bottom.",
  468: "A slow, heavy shark covered in large, thorn-like scales that look like brambles. It probably hunts by creeping up on prey and sucking it in. Fewer than a few hundred have ever been recorded.",
  469: "A large squid that can reach two metres, with two light organs the size of lemons on its arm tips. It flashes them in bright bursts, probably to dazzle its prey or confuse predators. It was first filmed alive in 2005.",
  470: "A small, round, see-through squid with a tuft of short arms above its eyes that looks like a pig's snout. Its body holds a fluid rich in ammonia, which is lighter than seawater and keeps it afloat. It often drifts upside down.",
  471: "Carries a coiled, chambered shell inside its body, using it as a float to keep itself head-down in the water. A small light organ at its tail glows green. Its white shells wash up on tropical beaches far more often than the animal is ever seen.",
  472: "A transparent, barrel-shaped squid that pulls its head and arms into its body when threatened. Like other glass squids, it stores ammonia to stay buoyant. Its eyes have light organs underneath that hide their shadow from below.",
  473: "A jelly-clear octopus that drifts in the open ocean, its eyes set on short stalks pointing straight up. That lets it watch for prey silhouetted against the light above. Its body is so transparent that only its eyes and gut stand out.",
  474: "Its suckers have turned into small lights that glow blue-green, an adaptation found in no other octopus. It probably uses the glow to draw small crustaceans into its webbed arms. It lives just above the deep seafloor of the North Atlantic.",
  475: "A deep red jellyfish with four to seven thick, fleshy arms instead of long stinging tentacles. It was discovered in 2003 off California by a deep-sea vehicle. Nobody yet knows how it catches its food.",
  476: "One of the largest invertebrate predators of the deep, with a bell up to a metre across and four ribbon-like arms up to ten metres long. It has been seen only around a hundred times in more than a century. A small fish is often found living among its arms.",
  477: "A large, scarlet shrimp-like animal of the deep that, when threatened, spits out a cloud of glowing blue liquid. The light blinds or distracts its attacker while it escapes. It belongs to a group called mysids, most of which are tiny.",
  478: "Lives only on a few hydrothermal vents in the Indian Ocean. It builds scales and part of its shell from iron sulphides, the only known animal to use iron like armour. Bacteria living inside it provide nearly all its food.",
  479: "Swarms in thousands over the hottest chimneys of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. It has no normal eyes, but a light-sensitive patch on its back may pick up the faint glow of the vents. Bacteria living in its gill chamber feed it.",
  480: "A mussel that lives in dense beds around hydrothermal vents, where there is no sunlight at all. Its gills house bacteria that make food from the sulphide in the vent water. It can grow to nearly 20 cm.",
  481: "A silver ribbonfish that swims head-up, with a fan of red fins on its tail. It is related to the oarfish and is just as rarely seen alive. Its body is so thin and fragile that it often breaks when caught.",
  482: "A silver ribbonfish with a tall crest over its eyes. It has an ink sac near its gut that it can use to squirt a dark cloud, a trick usually seen only in squid. It lives in deep water around the world and occasionally washes ashore.",
  483: "A sea lily, an animal related to sea stars that stands on a long stalk and filters food from the current with its feathery arms. Its relatives have been on Earth for hundreds of millions of years. Some can detach from their stalk and crawl away when a predator comes.",
  484: "A small silver hatchetfish common in the deep scattering layer, the band of life that shows up on ship sonar like a false seafloor. Its mirrored sides and glowing belly hide it from predators. It rises toward the surface at night.",
  485: "A dark, big-headed fish whose large scales come off at the slightest touch, with bony ridges on its skull that form a crest. Its head is full of open sensory canals that feel movement in the dark water. It eats mostly small crustaceans.",
  486: "Fires a cloud of glowing sparks from a tube on its shoulder when threatened. The light may confuse predators or lure them away. It is related to the slickheads of the deep slope.",
  487: "Spreads its long, thread-like pelvic fins out around it like the strings of a net, and they may feel for prey in the dark. A glowing barbel hangs from its chin. It is small enough to fit in a cupped hand.",
  488: "A stubby dragonfish with a mouthful of crooked fangs and a glowing barbel under its chin. Rows of small lights run along its sides. It rises toward the surface at night to hunt lanternfish and shrimp.",
  489: "A soft-bodied cusk-eel of the abyssal plain, its body tapering to a fine point like a rat's tail. It swims slowly over the mud, picking up worms and small crustaceans. Cusk-eels include some of the deepest-living fish ever caught.",
  490: "The most common large fish on the abyssal plain, cruising slowly just above the mud. It is often the first fish to arrive at bait dropped from a ship. It can live for decades in water just above freezing.",
  491: "A pink, tadpole-shaped fish with no scales, built soft and loose for crushing depths. Snailfish hold the record for the deepest fish ever filmed, at over 8,300 metres. It feeds on small crustaceans.",
  492: "The deepest-living lizardfish, pale grey and propped on its fins on the abyssal floor. It can sit still for hours and then lunge at anything that comes within reach. Each one is both male and female, which helps where a mate is this hard to find.",
  493: "A relative of the sand hoppers on beaches, grown to over 30 cm in the cold of the deep trenches. It scavenges whatever falls to the bottom. Most of what is known about it comes from baited traps lowered from ships.",
  495: "Looks like a lump of crumbly mud, but it is a single living cell the size of a fist, wrapped in a house it builds from mud and fragments of shell. Small worms and crustaceans live in the crevices of that house. It is among the largest single cells known.",
  496: "Swarms in hundreds onto any carcass that reaches the abyss; baited cameras show them arriving within minutes and stripping a fish to the bone. It can gorge until its body swells with food and then go months without eating. Its red colour is invisible this deep.",
  497: "A deep scarlet prawn of the abyss. No red light reaches this far down, so to anything looking at it, it is simply black. It swims just above the seafloor, feeding on small animals and drifting debris.",
  498: "No scientist has described it. Divers who have glimpsed it say it disappears completely when it turns side-on, its flanks mirroring the blue around it. It may be a hatchetfish relative grown unusually large.",
  499: "Not in any record. It is a thin black eel that ripples bands of light down its body in slow waves, like a sign on a dark street. Nobody knows whether the light is a signal or a lure.",
  500: "Seen only on the walls of deep canyons. Its lure glows a dull orange, unlike the blue-green of every known anglerfish. That colour should be almost invisible this deep, which makes its purpose a puzzle.",
  501: "So transparent that its whole skeleton shows through, like a drawing in glass. It has the mirrored belly of a hatchetfish but little else in common with one. No specimen has ever reached a laboratory.",
  502: "A long-legged crab that cements manganese nodules from the abyssal plain onto its shell. The nodules make it hard to crush and almost impossible to tell from the seabed. It may carry the same nodules for decades.",
  503: "A skate of the vent fields that lays its egg cases in the warm water beside the chimneys. Eggs of related skates take years to hatch in the cold; here they may hatch in months. Only its egg cases had been seen until now.",
  504: "A hagfish with a crown of long white barbels around its mouth. It tastes the water with them from a metre away and finds carcasses before anything else does. Like other hagfish, it produces huge amounts of slime when grabbed.",
  505: "Lives only on the rim of the brine pool, a lake of heavy salt water on the seafloor. Anything that swims into the brine dies, and this eel darts in to pull out the bodies. How it survives those brief dips is unknown.",
  506: "A red squid three metres long that sheds a trail of glowing sparks as it drops through the water. The sparks may distract predators while it escapes into the dark. It has been filmed only once.",
  507: "A tripodfish that, unlike its relatives, walks across the soft mud of the trench, one stilt after another. It may have to keep moving to stop itself sinking in. It feeds on tiny animals drifting just above the floor.",
  508: "Every other dragonfish flickers its barbel light on and off. This one holds its light perfectly steady, which may make it look like a harmless drifting particle. Its prey would have no reason to swim away.",
  509: "A pale, soft fish four metres long, seen only at the very bottom of the deepest pit. Snailfish are the deepest-living fish known, but none comes close to this size. It is a mystery how something so large finds enough to eat this deep.",
  2102: "Sloane's viperfish is normally the size of a hand, with fangs too long to fit inside its mouth. It lures prey with a glowing spine on its back and strikes with its head thrown back. This one has grown to over a metre and a half, a size never recorded.",
  2103: "The black dragonfish female is long, black and toothy, with a glowing barbel on her chin; the male is a small, toothless creature that never eats as an adult. Its larvae have eyes on long stalks. This one is a female grown to nearly two metres, far beyond any known size.",
  2106: "The black swallower can take down fish more than twice its length, its stomach stretching to hold them. Sometimes the meal rots before it can be digested, and the gas floats the swallower to the surface. This one is longer than a person.",
  2109: "The giant grenadier is a large-headed rattail of the North Pacific slope, with a body that tapers to a thin point. It scavenges and hunts squid and fish near the bottom. This one is the biggest ever seen, longer than a rowing boat.",
  2110: "The tripod fish stands on three stilts of fin rays and faces the current, its pectoral fins held out to feel for drifting food. Each one is both male and female. This one stands taller than a person.",
  2146: "The dumbo octopus lives deeper than any other octopus and swims by flapping two ear-like fins. It swallows prey whole. This one is more than two metres across, many times the size of any known dumbo.",
  2150: "The vampire squid is a small deep-sea cephalopod that turns itself inside out when threatened and eats drifting marine snow. It is neither a squid nor an octopus. This one is the size of a person, which is unheard of.",
  2162: "The giant isopod is a scavenger of the deep seafloor, related to pill bugs, and can go years without eating. It can grow to about 50 cm. This one is far larger, the size of a small dog.",
};
